WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.540
This is the Technical Difficulties, we're playing Citation Needed.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.640
Joining me today, he reads books you know, it's Chris Joel.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.940
[mumbles] I've got something in my teeth.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.440
Everybody's favourite Gary Brannan, Gary Brannan.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.640
Insert power cable here.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.260
Where?

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.680
Oh, you know.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.460
You committed to that.  I didn't think you 'd go for that one.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.480
I didn't think you'd go right in it.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:20.200
Urgh!

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.340
And the bounciest man on the Internet, Matt Gray.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.700
Bienvenue, YouTube!

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.260
In front of me I have an article from Wikipedia and these folks can't see it.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.460
Every fact they get right is a point and a ding [ding].

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.180
And there's a special prize for a particularly good answers, which is...

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.660
And today we are talking about Project Cybersyn.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.800
Oh! Oh...! Oh... There are many cyber sins.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.880
Is it a campaign by the Church of England to absolve robots of their sins?

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.660
No, and it is spelt S Y N.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.400
Oh come on! This is a novel or something.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.840
Nope. This is entirely real. But I'll admit it sounds a little bit science fictiony.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.920
So is it sin or synthetic?

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.300
It's an abbreviation of something. I'll give you one point for that [ding].

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.080
Ah, synergy...

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.040
Point! [ding]

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:02.540
Wahey, uh-huh!

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.220
It is indeed a portmanteau of cybernetics and synergy.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.100
Is it a government policy somewhere?

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.020
Ooh! Yes, have a point [ding].

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.260
Does anyone want to guess where in the world...

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:13.800
AMERICA!

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.120
Which bit?

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.580
[bad Southern US accent] The South.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.020
Now... if I said the...

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.960
Whatever the answer was correct, is the one I meant.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.400
Yes Gary, have a point. It's in South America. [ding]

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:26.900
Yea!

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.640
Chile.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.880
No, I'm fine, I've got a jumper on.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.560
I deliberately didn't pronounce it 'chilly', just so you wouldn't do that joke.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:36.720
Sod it!

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.120
It's happening anyway. Deal with it.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.680
I say, to be fair, all that did was to make you sound really pretentious.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:42.320
Yes.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.800
[Posh accent] Chile.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.040
[Posh accent] Chile, I think you will find it's pronounced.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.200
I think you'll find it's pronounced "CHYLE".

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.180
That's what Jimi Hendrix says.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.820
That's what we does.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.680
It's not Voodoo Chilli. Or maybe it should be that.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.860
[Posh accent] It's Voodoo Chilé.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:57.500
[Posh accent] Voodoo Chilé?

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.520
[Posh accent] Voodoo Chilé.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:01:59.900
You know it's one of those sleeveless jackets.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.620
But you become a zombie when you put it on.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:03.920
A voodoo gilet!

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:08.360
I'm so puffy and warm and... BRAINS!

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.580
Chile is the far left of bottom America, right?

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.940
It is. For most of the way down.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:13.860
Bottom America!

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.900
Far left of bottom America. You navigate like I do.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.280
It's the long one, isn't it?

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:18.780
Yes.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.840
It's the long thin one that goes all the way down one side.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.600
We went to school you know.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.060
About when are we talking about? When would you expect to see...

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:25.680
Lunch time!

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.480
2:30!

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.840
Two men swinging at the same ball there, eh?

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.080
It's like one of those out takes from a baseball game, where you see two people running for the same ball.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:39.740
What decade...

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.480
would you expect to hear Project Cybersyn?

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.120
-- If this isn't '80s, I am disappointed.
-- 70s?

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.360
You're going to be disappointed, it's the '70s. Have a point, Matt [ding]

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.480
Are we making South American robots?

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.540
1971 to 1973.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.320
Is that when they took over and killed everyone?

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.100
No...

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.740
South America in that era. That's a dubious joke to be making.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.640
That's what ended Project Cybersyn, was a military coup

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.520
that destroyed the control room.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.300
So yes... but(!)

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.360
It was a military [pigeon noise]

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.480
Military coo...

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.960
Pigeons are coming! Release the robots!

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.560
Sir, the robots aren't finished. The pigeons are pecking them to pieces! Oh, what a world!

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.520
Sir. The robots...

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.240
...ARE pigeons.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.200
[Pigeon coos]
-- Oh my god!

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.340
I can't take my glasses off, I'm wearing contacts.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.300
[Pigeon coos] [Robotic buzz]

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:22.980
My God!

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:23.940
[Robotic buzz] [Pigeon coos]

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.560
[Quietly] I don't like this.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:26.980
What I'd like in a robot pigeon is two things.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.440
One, someone would have to spend ages perfecting that pigeon walk...

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.840
so that their heads go back and forwards at the same speed they walk still.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.980
That's easy. You just put another linkage across their of their neck and have it link to the back of their forehead.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.500
That's a modern gyro that's got to try and not compensate for that.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.340
And the other is, they'll still make it so they pick up cigarette butts.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.000
Every pigeon in the square, moving in unison.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.640
Aargh!

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:46.320
That would be creepy.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.080
Oh, we've gone too far now.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:49.440
Laser eyes.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.760
Yeah, natch. Obviously. They can't hold one, they've got wings dammit!

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.520
It's not going to flap its wings to fly. It's going to have a rocket up its bum.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.620
For those of you just tuning in, Project Cybersyn...
is not robot pigeons.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.400
I can just see a whole Trafalgar Square of pigeons...

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.000
just stopping, wings extending and just them all launching upwards...

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.220
in synergy.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:13.020
Now that's a beaut... compared to them all turning round to look at you in synergy, that's a beautiful image.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.400
Project Cybersyn... gentlemen.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.720
Robot pigeons, wasn't it?

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.500
Robot, rocket-arsed pigeons.
We've confirmed this already, Tom.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:21.820
Chile.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.740
1970s... early 1970s as well.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.620
Was it a public computing project?

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.420
Ooh!

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.000
You can have a point [ding].
But what do you mean by public?

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.300
A terminal in a cafe for someone -- for everyone to use.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.340
No. Not quite.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:40.080
One of these state sponsored, productivity drives, that is going to put robots in factories.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.820
To make things more efficient.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.220
Yeah, you keep doing this... 'Is it a state sponsored productivity... robots... in that...'

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.940
No.... no.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.180
State sponsored. You know I will give you a point. State sponsored productivity [ding]. Because, you know, socialist government then.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.020
What were they trying to do?

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.100
Get everyone computing?

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.860
Teach computer literacy?

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.340
Yeah, but that's too early. That's '70s.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.180
Way too early.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.340
The thing then was all about improving productivity in workplaces...

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.220
because that's where places ended up with, like, robotic shop floors and stuff.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.780
So is it to do with shipping and transport and integrated movement of goods and stuff like that?

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.240
Yes! Have a point [ding]

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:15.380
Databases isn't it?

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.320
And you have a point as well [ding].

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.600
It was an attempt to plan the economy of Chile....

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:23.220
Using one massive computer system.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.680
Now! As this had a very short period of time working.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.560
Do I assume it wasn't entirely successful?

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.200
And everyone ended up burning down?

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.160
No, it did actually have a bit of success.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.940
Did it declare humans as 'no longer essential'?

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:42.260
No, they managed to switch it on. To look at the errors in the code.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:45.200
The screen flashes 'you're better off with a coup'.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:51.740
I know nothing about the politicals here. I don't know if that's even appropriate.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.260
"We've turned it on and all the screen says is 'Death!'"

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:59.060
All the state-run factories were given a computer terminal

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.060
where they typed all their details of what was happening.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.220
And that got fed back to...?

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.940
The president's office.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:06.980
The mainframe.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.560
A fellow in very small glasses with a slide rule.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:11.780
Matt's closer [ding].

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:12.420
America!

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.920
No. It was a central control room in Chile.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:17.960
Complete with, what?

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.180
Coffee machine?

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:20.000
Toilets?

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:21.480
There was a lot of manu...

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.200
If this was the '70s, a strike!

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.240
I'll tell you what, have a point [ding].

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.960
It helped deal with striking truck workers.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.080
By killing them?

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.240
No. By...

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:33.900
No.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.700
"Computer, what is our solution?"

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.620
"It just says 'Death!' again."

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.260
"Whatever I press!"

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:44.900
Striking truck drivers blocked all the access streets going to the capital.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.340
But they were able to work out what routes were free.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.740
What factories still had some workers that could send things out. And they used this central computer for it.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:53.260
Oh, it had Google Maps?

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.420
Not really back then?

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:56.440
No! Bing!

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:58.640
Oh God!

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.880
"Ah! I see you want to visit...
the centre of the Atlantic Ocean."

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.800
"Here is a picture of France."

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:05.880
Thanks Bing!

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:06.760
Thanks Bing!

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.600
Bing would be so much better if it was voiced by Bing Crosby.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.840
"So, I see you wanna find out more 'bout York in England do you...?"

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.280
"Well son, here you go."

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.780
Except it's Bing, So you'd just be given a map of the New York tube system.

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.600
"Son, why d'ya wanna go anywhere else?"

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:26.880
"Ah sir, that's all ya need to know about York... uh... you're not there."

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.240
"Swell game, fellas."

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:33.320
What did the central planning office have for the seven people, that were sat around...

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:33.940
Seats?

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:37.020
Super cool, lean back consoles with many screens and...

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.300
I'm going to give you the point [ding]. They had...

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.860
This is the '70s, whirling tape and lights, surely?

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:41.700
Oh yeah!

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:46.500
There we go. 1970s, Star Trek, science fiction style, command console chairs

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:51.200
for the seven people who took all this information and made the decisions.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.520
A sign on the toilet, saying 'Captain's Log'.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:56.440
Aww...

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.300
Which was fine, apart from the chaps in the very short dresses.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.180
Oh yeah!

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.720
-- I'm thinking Star Trek uniforms here.
-- Yeah.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:07.200
Early Next Generation Star Trek had gender neutral uniforms

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.280
that were basically short dresses, down to about...

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.500
I believe they were called skants.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:11.560
Skants. Yes.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:14.080
I thought that sounds like a crisp based snack.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:14.900
Skant!

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:16.520
-- Skants!
-- New Skants!

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:19.160
You open the bag -- there's not much in.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:22.220
Buy them today...

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.460
BECAUSE THE COMPUTER TOLD YOU!

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.720
Buy them or face the Pigeons!

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:35.280
You know in history, there are these people who have these grand dreams of socialist planning and computers

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:36.740
that are never made real.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:38.740
This man's dream became real.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:39.720
William Shatner!

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.760
He just wanted a real life Enterprise.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:45.500
What country did Stafford Beer come from?

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:46.560
'Murica!

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:47.540
No.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:48.880
The United Kingdom!

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.160
Yes he did! [ding].

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:54.160
A British management cybernetics consultant.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.440
-- [BEEP]ing hell.
-- What? In 70s...

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.480
-- In 1970s.
-- You can't be that any more, can you?

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.000
Stafford Beer.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.980
You have to be a startup to be one of those now.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.860
He also sympathised with the Chilean socialists.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.120
And that worked quite well!

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.240
I imagine it did.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.760
He basically got, I don't know if it was a phone call in those days, but he got a message, out of the blue.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:16.360
-- Probably a telex.
-- A telex, surely?

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:18.580
From the COM-PU-TER!

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:22.760
From the wonderfully named Fernando Flores and he basically dropped all his other work and said

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:23.960
"Yes, I will go to Chile!"

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.600
Someone picked it up later. It would be all over the floor.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.120
And help the socialists.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:29.280
Did they have a five year plan?

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:31.540
Well they did. But it lasted about two years.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:33.400
I was going to say, did that not work out?

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.040
That's the duration of most five year plans, in my experience. Actually, yeah.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.080
Let me guess, they ****ed off, quite soon after.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:40.040
No, they were given a military coup.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:42.580
Given one?! ♫ Happy Birthday to you...

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:44.080
♫ Here's a military coup... ♫

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.560
♫ You've five minutes to leave

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:49.280
♫ And your family too! ♫

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:58.880
I mean Chris, you pressed the button there, but it was entirely justified.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:01.760
Those are biscuits for Gary. Well done.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:07.220
Yes. The 1973 Chilean coup d'etat.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.420
Aren't those the little things you have at parties?

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.540
-- That's... that's...
-- Crudités!

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:13.880
A crudités coup d'etat!

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.580
Ooh look! It's shaped like a tiny rifle.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:19.240
How avant-garde!

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.320
Ooh! It's taken over my intestines!

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:24.360
Yes, the caviar was somewhat suspect.

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:29.200
Opens the fridge... Flags... Parade...

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.520
Tins of pilchards going round like nuclear missiles.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:37.260
Than, many years later, people are wearing red t-shirts, with crudités on them.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.060
Big pictures of a tin of sardines...

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.740
Being held. They were obviously airbrushed, to make them look a bit newer.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:46.540
Yeah!

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:49.220
The smoked salmon ones are at the top

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:52.820
because although all men are born equal, some are more equal than others.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:56.800
Apart from one really old one in the middle. Looks like it could fall off at any time.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.560
With an awesome mustache.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.660
It's a Chilean coup, so with a big hat, sunglasses.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.160
Who backed the Chilean coup? In 1973?

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:05.540
The COM-PU-TER did!

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:06.780
It's got to be the States.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.320
It's go to be the States. Richard Nixon's government. Yes [ding].

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.960
There is a name you might recognise, that came to power afterwards.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.440
-- Pinochet!
-- Pinochet! [ding]

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.340
Dammit! I would have been right. Ah well.

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:17.880
It was the coup in which Pinochet came to power.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:19.320
And... you know...

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:26.200
As part of this, the massive infrastructure of socialism that Nixon and the U.S. were fighting against.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:30.120
This massive computer orchestrated thing... destroyed.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.980
It's worth pointing out, in less than...

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:34.120
Ooh hoo hoo hoo!

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.720
I didn't actually intend for that to connect.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:38.360
-- Hel-lo!
-- Handbags at dawn ladies!

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:40.920
He's right, it was worth pointing out.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:44.680
That is the most 'shut that door' slap I've ever seen, Tom.

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:46.060
It really is.

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:51.840
It's worth mentioning that they actually did get to advanced prototype in under two years.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:56.020
Which for a central government operation -- like, I don't know if Britain could do that

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.560
with one thing right now.
Let alone a big national project.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.480
So they went full-on startup?

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:02.580
Yeah. They...

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:06.520
They had a great idea. They burned out after two years, almost literally.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.120
That's it. That's all I'm saying.

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.460
Yeah, they managed it.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:13.480
And no-one really got any money at the end. And the person whose idea it was,

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:17.340
despite the fact it was technically a failure, went back and had a successful career doing other things.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:18.360
And no-one's heard about it now!

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.320
Basically a tech startup. There you go.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.480
The late '90s, documented, right there.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:23.180
Yes!

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.300
So, congratulations at the end of that. I think Gary, you...

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:28.080
You very clearly win this one.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:35.080
You win a restaurant that cooks lingerie over a box of hot coals, that was previously owned by a famous civil engineer...

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:38.000
Go on...

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.440
It's Brunel's Brassieres and Braziers Brasserie.

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:44.260
So congratulations, enjoy that.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.980
With that we say good bye to Chris Joel...

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:48.260
To Gary Brannan...

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:49.040
To Matt Gray...

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:50.520
I've been Tom Scott and we'll see you next time.

